Yes. This is a **very good feature opportunity for REA**, and I would avoid creating multiple copies of essentially the same template.

For your example:

> **Hello and welcome to WinBucks. My name is {agent_name}. How may I help you today?**

an agent might frequently want only:

> **Hello and welcome to WinBucks.**

or:

> **My name is {agent_name}. How may I help you today?**

The key is to distinguish between a **Template** and a **Template Snippet**.

### 1. Keep one master template

Store only:

> **Hello and welcome to WinBucks. My name is {agent_name}. How may I help you today?**

Then allow an agent to select a portion of it when needed.

There are several ways REA could do this.

---

## Option A — "Use Part of Template" ⭐ My recommendation

When an agent opens a template, give them:

```text
Hello and welcome to WinBucks.
My name is {agent_name}.
How may I help you today?

[ Use Entire Template ]

[ Use Selected Text ]
```

If they choose **Use Selected Text**, the template becomes selectable.

For example:

```text
Hello and welcome to WinBucks.
^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

My name is {agent_name}.
How may I help you today?
```

The agent highlights the part they want and clicks:

> **Use Selection**

REA then copies only that text into the response/composer.

### Why I like this

You don't need:

```text
Template 1:
Hello and welcome to WinBucks.

Template 2:
My name is {agent_name}. How may I help you today?

Template 3:
Hello and welcome to WinBucks. My name is...
```

You maintain **one source of truth**.

---

# Option B — Make sentences individually selectable

This could be even nicer for your particular use case.

REA could display:

> **Hello and welcome to WinBucks.**
> **My name is {agent_name}.**
> **How may I help you today?**

When the agent hovers over a sentence:

```text
Hello and welcome to WinBucks.          [＋]
My name is {agent_name}.                [＋]
How may I help you today?               [＋]
```

Clicking `＋` adds that sentence to the response.

You could also allow multiple selections:

```text
☑ Hello and welcome to WinBucks.
☑ My name is {agent_name}.
☐ How may I help you today?
```

Then:

> **Insert Selected**

This is essentially creating **temporary snippets from the template without saving them as separate templates**.

---

# Option C — Copy a selected portion

The simplest implementation is probably:

**Highlight → Right click → Copy Selection**

But I wouldn't make that the primary REA feature.

The user already has that capability in the browser.

The advantage of REA is that it can do something more intelligent:

> **Highlight → Insert into Response**

---

# Option D — "Quick Insert" sentences

There is another approach that I think would fit REA particularly well.

When viewing a template:

```text
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Welcome Message                             │
│                                             │
│ Hello and welcome to WinBucks.              │
│ My name is {agent_name}.                    │
│ How may I help you today?                   │
│                                             │
│ ─────────────────────────────────────────── │
│ Quick Actions                               │
│                                             │
│ [ Insert Entire Message ]                   │
│ [ Insert Greeting ]                         │
│ [ Insert Introduction ]                     │
│ [ Insert Question ]                         │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

But there's a catch:

**"Greeting", "Introduction", "Question" are semantic labels.**

Someone has to define those sections.

That may be useful eventually, but I wouldn't make it necessary for your first implementation.

---

# I think there's an even better hybrid for REA

I'd give each template **two levels**:

### Master Template

The complete message:

> Hello and welcome to WinBucks. My name is {agent_name}. How may I help you today?

### Selectable Content

The agent can:

**1. Use entire template**

or

**2. Select text**

or

**3. Select sentence(s)**

So the interface could be:

```text
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Welcome Message                                  │
│                                                  │
│ Hello and welcome to WinBucks.                   │
│ My name is {agent_name}.                         │
│ How may I help you today?                        │
│                                                  │
│ ──────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│                                                  │
│ [ Insert Entire Template ]                      │
│ [ Select Part of Template ]                     │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

When **Select Part** is clicked:

```text
SELECT WHAT YOU NEED

☑ Hello and welcome to WinBucks.
☑ My name is {agent_name}.
☐ How may I help you today?

             [ Insert Selected ]
```

That's very intuitive for agents.

---

# There's one important consideration: variables

Your `{agent_name}` example makes this particularly interesting.

REA should **resolve variables before insertion**.

Suppose the template is:

> Hello and welcome to WinBucks. My name is {agent_name}. How may I help you today?

and the logged-in agent is:

> Sarah

REA should turn it into:

> **Hello and welcome to WinBucks. My name is Sarah. How may I help you today?**

If the agent selects only:

> My name is {agent_name}.

REA should insert:

> **My name is Sarah.**

So the variable system should operate on **snippets/selections too**, not only complete templates.

---

# And I would NOT save these selections

This is important given your original requirement.

Suppose Sarah selects:

> Hello and welcome to WinBucks.

REA should **not** automatically create:

```text
Template #184
"Hello and welcome to WinBucks."
```

Instead, it's a **temporary selection derived from Template #12**.

Conceptually:

```text
MASTER TEMPLATE
       │
       ├── Entire message
       │
       ├── Selected sentence
       │
       └── Selected passage
```

Only the master template is stored.

---

# One feature I think would be especially powerful later

### "Save Selection as Snippet"

Although selections shouldn't automatically be saved, you could optionally let an agent say:

> **Save this selection for quick access**

Then REA creates a **Snippet**, not another Template.

For example:

**Template**

> Welcome Message

**Snippet**

> "Hello and welcome to WinBucks."

This distinction becomes useful:

### Template

A complete, structured response.

### Snippet

A reusable piece of a response.

That gives you a much cleaner content architecture:

```text
Templates
   ↓
Complete responses

Snippets
   ↓
Reusable pieces of responses

Variables
   ↓
Dynamic information
```

---

## My recommendation for REA

I'd implement it in this order:

**Phase 1 — Essential**

> **Use Entire Template**
> **Select Text → Insert Selection**

**Phase 2 — Convenience**

> Sentence-level selection / checkboxes.

**Phase 3 — Power feature**

> **Save Selection as Snippet**

That would solve your problem without flooding the template database with hundreds of tiny duplicated messages.

And importantly, it fits the nature of REA very well: **the template library remains organized around complete, approved responses, while agents still have the flexibility to use only the portion they actually need.**
